{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA (Phase 1) confirmed before repair.\n\nStep 0(a): duplicate/similar sweep found this ticket as the active report; related closed bug 05cb-f7af-2d85-4d60 fixed `show` visibility for inbound blockers but not `next_batch` scheduling. Open/in-progress bug list confirms c1ef-66e6-9aaa-4c98 is the active scheduling defect.\nStep 0(b): no explicit reported build in the report; created_at 1788332604923951551 gives floor a85cb4781845ccf0d8c0375adce9a01391ba4bc8. Suspect paths had no commits between that floor and current origin/main.\nStep 0(c): debug worktree is current at 384ca0dc2d7ceaabe2f27575dac2936c3a1e86cd (= origin/main).\n\nDesign-decision check: no ADR found that intentionally gives `next_batch` looser readiness than `deps`/`ready`. Authoritative docs say `ready` lists tickets ready to work when all blockers are closed, and `ticket-model.md` states the hierarchy is what `ready`/`next-batch` operate on and that a ticket's blocked-ness is an inbound `blocks` or outgoing `depends_on`, consistent with what `ready`/`next-batch` compute. So the two views should agree; fixing `next_batch` to honor documented blocking-edge semantics is a defect fix, not a design change.\n\nMechanism: `src/rebar/_engine_support/next_batch.py::compute` builds ready candidates and `_blocked_ids` by inspecting only each ticket's outgoing `depends_on` edges. A prerequisite recorded as `A blocks B` is stored as an outbound edge on A and an inbound edge on B, so B has no outgoing `depends_on` and is incorrectly admitted to `next_batch`. `ticket_deps` reaches `src/rebar/graph/_blockers.py::_find_direct_blockers`, which combines B's own outgoing `depends_on` with other tickets' outbound `blocks` targeting B, so it correctly reports B as blocked.\n\nDiscriminating local reproduction: a constructed store with epic/story/blocker/blocked and one LINK on blocker (`relation=blocks`, `target_id=blocked`) produced `ticket_deps('blocked') = blockers:['blocker'], ready_to_work:false` while `next_batch('epic')` returned `['blocker','blocked']` in batch. The same store using `blocked depends_on blocker` excluded `blocked` from `next_batch`, isolating the divergence to inbound `blocks` handling rather than status filtering, descendants, or file-overlap scheduling.\n\nDefect origin: attribution is scope-of-work/omission in the next-batch Python port ticket `sure-beech-taunt` / `1ff3-a12e-f99a-4b07`. The current faulty lines mostly blame to commit 92caa823ae17e (`feat(next-batch): port to Python behind REBAR_COMPUTE (Tier C, sure-beech-taunt)`), which faithfully carried the earlier bash selector's outgoing-only `depends_on` filter. That ticket's acceptance/port parity work should have covered the documented `blocks`/`depends_on` equivalence and the `deps`/`next_batch` readiness invariant. The commit predates the trailer convention (`verify-commit-ticket --rev 92caa823ae17e` finds no resolvable ticket), so I will record the origin explicitly on close with `--caused-by=1ff3-a12e-f99a-4b07` rather than relying on blame auto-derivation."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788338030107191920,"uuid":"3f56a100-b6bd-44c9-9eaf-a85d5c3c58cb"}